PART 0 -- primitive self-tests and THIS round's guard
==============================================================================
  primitives + (FAR) evaluator agree with hand computation on P3/P4/P6/C7/C8/Petersen

  GUARD.  CLASS CLAIMED: any evaluator that decides whether a host CAN carry a
  condition-4 vertex from the SIZE of its centre instead of from the PLACEMENT of
  its centre.  This is not r34's zero-step, r35's one-step, r36's late-block,
  r37's off-by-one/anchor-drift, r38's cost-evaluator, r39's centre-widen/quantifier-
  swap/stratum-offset, r40's sphere-shift, r41's table-trust/peri-as-noncentral, nor
  r42's diam-for-ecc/mean-deg-for-l.  It is the defect brief E46's own section 3
  fell into, and this round's whole method is the distinction it erases.

  (D9) COUNT-FOR-PLACEMENT: decide 'host may carry condition 4' by (CS-1'), i.e. by
       |Ctr| <= max_{w not in Ctr} |S_rad(w)|, instead of by the correct test
       'the intersection of the spheres S_rad(c) over c in Ctr is non-empty'.
  ONE-SIDED BY A THEOREM: if some w satisfies condition 4 then Ctr is contained in
  S_rad(w), so |Ctr| <= |S_rad(w)| <= max_w |S_rad(w)|.  So (D9) can only OVER-fire,
  never miss -- and a guard that can only over-fire is worthless unless it actually
  DOES over-fire on live data.  Measured below.

